---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import Landscape from '@/components/tools/calc2/Landscape.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="mulch-calculator">
  <Landscape client:load variant="mulch" />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the mulch calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      The mulch calculator multiplies the area of the bed by the depth of mulch to get the volume, then
      divides the volume by the size of one bag and rounds up to whole bags.
    </p>
    <pre><code>area         = length × width          (or π × (diameter ÷ 2)² for a circle)
volume (ft³) = area (ft²) × depth (in) ÷ 12
volume (yd³) = volume (ft³) ÷ 27
bags         = volume ÷ bag size, rounded up</code></pre>

    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      A bed of 100 square feet with 3 inches of mulch takes 100 × 3 ÷ 12 = 25 cubic feet, which is
      0.93 cubic yards. That is 12.5 bags of 2 cubic feet, so buy 13, or 9 bags of 3 cubic feet.
    </p>

    <h2>Coverage of one bag</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Depth</th><th>2 ft³ bag</th><th>3 ft³ bag</th><th>1 yd³ bulk</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="tabular-nums">
        <tr><td>1 in</td><td>24 ft²</td><td>36 ft²</td><td>324 ft²</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2 in</td><td>12 ft²</td><td>18 ft²</td><td>162 ft²</td></tr>
        <tr><td>3 in</td><td>8 ft²</td><td>12 ft²</td><td>108 ft²</td></tr>
        <tr><td>4 in</td><td>6 ft²</td><td>9 ft²</td><td>81 ft²</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>The animation</h2>
    <p>
      The bed is drawn to scale from above. The mulch spreads across it one bag at a time, and the counter
      under the drawing shows which bag is being spread.
    </p>

    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Bagged mulch is often compressed and fluffs up when spread; loose bulk mulch settles by a few per cent over the first weeks.</li>
      <li>Plants, stepping stones and edging take up area. Subtract large ones from the bed size.</li>
      <li>For a slope, use the area along the slope, which is larger than the area on the plan.</li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
